\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



A sum of two squares means $a^2+b^2$ with $a,b\in{\mathbb{Z}}$, including a zero summand. The asymptotic interval question concerns sufficiently large real $X$ and the interval with endpoints $X-X^{1/4}/10$ and $X$. The constant $1/10$ is part of the target, not an unspecified positive constant. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07380}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there always a sum of two squares between $X - \frac{1}{10}X^{1/4}$ and $X$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Scope and interpretation.}

The source background concerns a large-$X$ gap bound. This is the asymptotic reading; the wording always is not used to assert the false small-$X$ version. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Improve the uniform one-sided constant to 1/10, or prove any o(X\textasciicircum{}{1/4}) worst-case gap bound. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must every sufficiently large number have a sum of two squares within one tenth of its fourth root immediately below it?

